#!/usr/bin/env etcs
#IMPORT ACE_ROOT/modules

# The w/a/s/d edge: the window's event stream, consumed by a 3D scene.
#
#     detach scene3d_input.etcs window=<Window> scene=<Drawable3D>
#
# THE TWO ENDS ARE ON DIFFERENT ENTITIES, in different modules, and neither
# was written knowing about the other. WindowProvider produces InputEvents
# because a window is an InputSource; RenderProvider consumes them because a
# scene wants to be moved. What crosses is the ontology's own event type
# (ontology/InputSource.h), which is why no compile-time relationship between
# the two modules exists or is needed. The pair is built on the CONSUMER and
# the producer is handed in (core/CommandExecutor.h), which is exactly what
# makes that possible.
#
# IT REPLACES window_events.etcs RATHER THAN JOINING IT. ProduceEvents is
# where PollEvents gets called -- it is the OS message pump as well as the
# event source -- so this edge is what keeps the window alive, and the cross
# still ends the closure through it. Run both and you get two observers on the
# ring, which works, and two pumps, which is a waste.
#
# CONTROLS, and the direction is deliberate: the SCENE moves, not the camera.
# What a projection can see is the relative position of the two, and moving the
# scene means one translation at the root relocates the whole tree (scene3d.etcs
# on why).
#
#     W / S   forward / back, along the way the camera is facing
#     A / D   strafe left / right
#     Q / E   up / down
#     mouse   look -- composed onto the scene's own row 3 (OrderVector.h)
#             and applied to whichever camera projects it
#     ctrl+tab  release the captured cursor
#
# The keys are stated as the VIEWER moves, and the scene moves opposite; the
# direction is relative to the current yaw, so W follows the mouse rather than
# a world axis. Pitch aims the view and does not lift the feet, which is why
# Q/E exist.
#
# BOTH KINDS ARRIVE ON THIS ONE EDGE. Keys and pointer deltas share a record
# (ontology/InputSource.h) and one handler, because a look and a step that
# happened together are one intent and two streams would let them drift apart.
#
# The consumer only RECORDS -- a bit per key, two angles for the look. The
# motion is integrated where it is observed, in the projection, over the
# interval since the last frame: an event says a key CHANGED, and movement
# depends on what is HELD, which are different questions. Answering the second
# from the first is what makes W+A a diagonal rather than a race between two
# key-repeat timers, and what makes the speed the scene's rather than the
# keyboard's.
#
# Mouse capture is the CALLER's to set (main.CaptureMouse(1), see
# render_scene3d.etcs) and the WINDOW's to release (Ctrl+Tab): whether the
# cursor is free is a property of the window and of what else the user is doing
# with it, not of this edge -- and the way out must keep working even when this
# edge does not.

requires window [Window]
requires scene [Drawable3D]

# TWO CHANNELS, TWO THREADS. Keys and pointer share nothing that needs
# synchronising -- a step is a function of which keys are held, a look of where
# the pointer is -- so they travel separately and neither queues behind the
# other's bursts (ontology/InputSource.h). An edge blocks the thread that
# states it, so the pointer half is detached and this thread keeps the keys.
#
# The keys edge is also the window's event pump, which is why it is the one
# that ends the closure when the window closes; the pointer edge only drains.
detach scene3d_look.etcs window=window scene=scene

window.ProduceEvents() -> scene.ConsumeInput()

# The producer returns when the window closes, which is also when the cross was
# pressed. Closing here is the same acknowledgement window_events.etcs makes:
# the closure that opened the window is the one that ends.
window.Close()
